WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:05.984
If you're not getting the responses you want from LLMs,

00:00:05.984 --> 00:00:12.172
or "Large Language Models", like the model that powers chatGPT,

00:00:12.172 --> 00:00:14.136
I may know what's wrong.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:15.830
It might be you.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:16.630
No, no, no.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:17.500
Hear me out.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:23.520
Look, you see, the way that we prompt these large language models is very important.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:30.959
So prompting plays a significant impact in the quality of the response that the LLM will generate.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.269
Let's take a look at an example.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.511
I'm working on a homework assignment for my Econ 101 class, and I need some help.

00:00:38.696 --> 00:00:42.435
So, I issue the following prompt to a large language model.

00:00:42.552 --> 00:00:45.561
Question: "explain the different types of banks."

00:00:45.561 --> 00:00:48.371
The LLM responds with this,

00:00:48.371 --> 00:00:52.860
"Banks along a river can take various forms depending on whether they are natural or artificial".

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.439
Whoa ho, hang on there!

00:00:55.830 --> 00:01:00.744
Here I am trying to understand the difference between a credit union and an investment institution

00:01:00.744 --> 00:01:02.915
and it's talking to me about river banks!

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:07.644
This is an example of a particular type of prompt,

00:01:07.644 --> 00:01:13.369
and that is called "zero-shot" prompting.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:17.605
You're providing the model with a single question or instruction

00:01:17.605 --> 00:01:20.849
without any additional context, examples, or guidance.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:26.136
The model is expected to understand and answer the prompt without that context,

00:01:26.136 --> 00:01:32.423
and to do so, it relies solely on its preexisting knowledge and its ability to generalize from that knowledge

00:01:32.423 --> 00:01:34.844
to generate a relevant and accurate response.

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:39.910
And as you can see, it can lead to some suboptimal responses.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.150
Now, "bank" is a homograph.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.919
It has multiple meanings.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:50.775
One method to clear that ambiguity up is to employ a different type of prompting

00:01:50.775 --> 00:01:55.060
called "few-shot" prompting.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:58.660
Now, here is an example of few-shot prompting.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.255
So, we've got a question: "what is the primary function of a bank?"

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:04.578
Answer: "A bank's primary function is

00:02:04.578 --> 00:02:08.830
to accept deposits, provide loans and offer other financial services to individuals and businesses."

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:11.627
Question: "explain the different types of bank."

00:02:11.627 --> 00:02:15.785
With few-shot prompting, the model is provided with one or more examples

00:02:15.785 --> 00:02:18.639
to help guide its understanding of the task at hand.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.509
By providing an example related to financial institutions,

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:29.145
the LLM is more likely to understand that you are asking about types of bank in the context of finance...

00:02:29.626 --> 00:02:32.710
...rather than the stream at the bottom of your garden.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:37.840
Now, in this example, we could probably just have used a better zero-shot prompt.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.790
Like, "explain the different types of banking financial institutions."

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:43.960
That probably would have worked.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:47.889
But few-shot prompting has other advantages too.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:53.600
It can help an LLM understand the expected format a response should take.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:03:00.520
Like this, so we've got question: "create a title from my web page, then a title tag with all of our banks".

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.295
Then, question: "create a heading for my article."

00:03:03.295 --> 00:03:08.134
Then we have an H1 title pair with types of banks in those tags,

00:03:08.134 --> 00:03:10.830
and then we say, "question: list the types of banks."

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:16.593
And here the LLM may derive "we are looking for answers in HTML notation"

00:03:16.593 --> 00:03:20.198
and respond accordingly like this.

00:03:20.198 --> 00:03:25.590
Now there's another way that few-shot prompting can help, and that is to aid reasoning.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:30.973
So let's take an example from the paper, "Large Language Models Are Zero-Shot Reasoners"

00:03:30.973 --> 00:03:34.975
which was written by the University of Tokyo and Google Research.

00:03:35.097 --> 00:03:39.986
And they issued this zero-shot prompt to a large language model.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.069
Question: "a juggler can juggle 16 balls.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:46.939
Half of the balls are golf balls, and half of the golf balls are blue.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.936
How many blue golf balls are there?"

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.041
The answer is...

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:53.520
now, can you figure this out?

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:58.205
It's not too tricky, but it was for the LLM.

00:03:58.557 --> 00:03:59.218
"8"

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.000
Wrong!

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.729
So next in the paper they tried a few-shot prompt.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:08.379
So we start off with a sample question and a sample completion.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.050
So, question: "Roger has five tennis balls.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.060
He buys two more cans of tennis balls.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.159
Each can has three tennis balls.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:16.750
How many tennis balls does he have now?".

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.269
Answer: the answer is 11.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.630
Question: "a juggler can juggle 16 balls..." and so forth.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:29.460
Now we've shown the model of what a right answer looks like by applying addition and multiplication to a sentence.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:34.809
So, did the a few-shot prompt get us the right answer?

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:36.990
Eight, again!

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:37.990
No!

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:45.899
But by making a slight change, we can improve the reasoning of the LLM and get the right answer.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:52.410
And we can apply that change to either few-shot prompts, or to zero-shot prompts.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.490
So, what is this mysterious addition?

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:07.086
Well, it's called "chain of thought", or CoT,

00:05:07.086 --> 00:05:10.319
and to invoke it, just add wording such as,

00:05:10.319 --> 00:05:12.354
"let's think step by step."

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:17.790
Effectively, we've asked the LLM to document its thinking.

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.939
We're asking to see its chain of thought.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:22.669
And here's what we get.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.000
So the LLM responds to us with, "there are 16 balls in total.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.769
Half of the balls are golf balls.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.430
That means there are eight golf balls.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:31.959
Half of the golf balls are blue.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.666
That means there are four golf balls."

00:05:34.666 --> 00:05:36.660
Four golf balls!

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:38.909
That is the right answer!

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:45.779
Now, this particular test was applied to the InstructGPT model, which is a couple of years old.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.372
Newer models like GPT-4

00:05:48.372 --> 00:05:53.789
can invoke mathematical reasoning without the "let's think" step-by-step chain-of-thought prompting.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:06:01.470
But chain-of-thought prompting remains a valuable tool in prompt engineering for a number of reasons.

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:07.260
Reason number one is it encourages the model to provide a more detailed,

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:12.000
and specifically, a more transparent response.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:17.490
And an explanation of that response and its reasoning process.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:22.872
And this helps users better understand how the model arrived at a particular answer,

00:06:22.872 --> 00:06:26.819
making it easier for them to evaluate the correctness and relevance of the response.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:33.647
And that's all an important part of XAI, or "Explainable AI".

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.840
And then reason number two for chain-of-thought prompting

00:06:37.957 --> 00:06:42.049
is that it can be used to improve the quality of a model's response

00:06:42.049 --> 00:06:48.167
by encouraging it to consider alternative perspectives.

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:51.349
Or different approaches.

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:57.933
And by asking the model to think through various possibilities, it can generate more well-rounded and comprehensive answers,

00:06:58.134 --> 00:07:02.869
which can be particularly valuable when dealing with open-ended or subjective questions.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:07.967
Look ultimately few-shot prompting and chain-of-thought prompting

00:07:07.967 --> 00:07:13.989
are powerful techniques that can be employed to improve the quality of responses generated by large language models.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:19.398
By providing the model with additional context, examples or guidance,

00:07:19.398 --> 00:07:21.940
users can help the model better understand the task at hand

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:27.404
and generate more accurate, relevant and well-reasoned responses.

00:07:27.404 --> 00:07:33.173
And not to mention, keep better track of how many golf balls we're juggling.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:36.299
If you have any questions, please drop us a line below.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:41.305
And if you want to see more videos like this in the future, please like and subscribe.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:42.905
Thanks for watching.

